  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nOn Friday the upper level Pacific system will be approaching from \nthe southwest. Ahead of it a few impulses of mid and low level \nvorticity are progged to pass over the area, allowing showers to \nexist over mostly the intermountain areas. In addition, a jet streak \nof about 80-100 knots intensifies ahead of the system. The phase of \nthe jet and impulses are somewhat mistimed to allow for more \nwidespread precipitation. The jet will suppress the development of \nprecipitation due to a healthy amount of positive omega below 500 \nmbar. The exceptions are in the Nebraska Panhandle where the \nentrance region of the jet may be more favorable. It also helps that \nthis region will have more moisture and instability to work with. \nSkew-T profiles have about 500 MUCAPE and 60 knots of speed shear to \nperhaps prop up a few thunderstorms at and after peak daytime \nheating. No severe weather is anticipated with any storm, though \nsmall hail is a probability given the shear. The clearing cloud \ncoverage to the east allows much of the Panhandle to reach the mid \n70 to low 80s in the afternoon. Moving west, the cloud coverage \nincreases, only allowing southeast Wyoming to make it into the mid \n60s to low 70s. Overnight a cold front is expected to make its way \nsoutheast through the area. A 10 degree drop from Thursday overnight \nlows is likely, with lows in the mid 30s to low 40s.\n\nReturning to the upper level system to our west. The evolution \nthrough Friday is for the trough to split and elongate. The northern \nportion gets picked up by a strong negatively tilted trough in \nCanada, while the southern end of the trough remains over the \nsouthwest. This southern lobe of the system will be the driver of \nmore exciting weather going into Sunday and Monday.\n\nPost front Saturday is anticipated to be breezy and partly to mostly \ncloudy. Winds behind the front could be fresh with gusts to 40 mph \npossible. A few showers are possible as they drift north from the \nadvection activity in Colorado, but otherwise a dry day. \nTemperatures struggle to make it to 60, with most of us staying in \nthe 50s. Sunday the remaining Pacific system moves northeast \nbringing with it more chances for precipitation. The GFS is picking \nup on a possible TROWL setting up over our northern counties. \nTemperatures are marginal for any accumulation of snow, but a mix of \nsnow and rain at the surface is possible. At our peaks an inch or \ntwo of snow may be possible.\n\nFollowing the system a ridge builds in behind it. Clearing skies and \na gradual warming trend. The next storm system is being advertised \nin the GFS, however there are significant run to run variability in \nthe timing and positioning of this system. The ECMWF is telling a \nsimilar story, although the system is much flatter and slower. \nPrecipitation chances would be on the lower side with this system \nwith not much synoptically to refill the moisture reserve ahead of \nthe trough and not much attendant with the airmass associated with \nit.",
